1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,609
A breakout. Buyers piled in. Plus 1,800 contracts of delta in one candle.

2
00:00:12,059 --> 00:00:16,578
Next candle, price was back inside. And those same buyers were trapped.

3
00:00:17,028 --> 00:00:22,592
Today: trapped traders, and how they become fuel for price.

4
00:00:23,592 --> 00:00:28,582
Hello, this is episode nineteen of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

5
00:00:29,031 --> 00:00:32,741
Last time: iceberg orders and spoofing.

6
00:00:33,191 --> 00:00:39,147
Today: trapped traders. This ties episodes sixteen and seventeen together.

7
00:00:40,147 --> 00:00:45,711
A trapped trader entered at the wrong place, and price moved against him right away.

8
00:00:46,161 --> 00:00:51,960
Now he has two choices: exit at a loss, or wait for his stop to hit.

9
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:55,728
Either way, he has to place the opposite order.

10
00:00:56,728 --> 00:01:03,493
A trapped buyer exits by selling. That selling pushes price further down.

11
00:01:03,943 --> 00:01:07,574
The more people trapped, the more fuel.

12
00:01:08,024 --> 00:01:13,902
And their stops pile up in one place. The liquidity pool from episode seventeen.

13
00:01:14,902 --> 00:01:22,137
Our example. Gold futures, in a range for three sessions: 2640 to 2660.

14
00:01:22,588 --> 00:01:28,701
Everyone is watching the top wall. Break 2660 and it is a breakout.

15
00:01:29,701 --> 00:01:34,612
Price went to 2664. Candle volume: 4,200.

16
00:01:35,062 --> 00:01:40,782
3,000 on the ask, 1,200 on the bid. Delta plus 1,800.

17
00:01:41,232 --> 00:01:47,345
Buyers bought between 2661 and 2664. Average around 2662.5.

18
00:01:48,345 --> 00:01:55,423
But no more buyers showed up. The next candle closed back inside the range at 2657.

19
00:01:55,874 --> 00:01:58,878
This candle had a delta of minus 1,100.

20
00:01:59,327 --> 00:02:04,579
The buyers from 2662.5 are now 5.5 points down per contract.

21
00:02:05,579 --> 00:02:09,705
Their stops were inside the range, below 2655.

22
00:02:10,156 --> 00:02:15,642
The stops hit, the selling came in. Price fell to 2648.

23
00:02:16,091 --> 00:02:22,858
That is the full cycle of a trap: get in, get stuck, get out, and push price.

24
00:02:23,858 --> 00:02:29,656
The face of a trap, three signs. One: big delta on the breakout.

25
00:02:30,107 --> 00:02:35,828
Two: price did not follow through, and soon closed back inside the range.

26
00:02:36,278 --> 00:02:43,043
Three: the next candle delta flips. The divergence from episode fourteen shows up here too.

27
00:02:44,043 --> 00:02:51,932
The same game runs in reverse. Price broke below 2640 to 2636, delta minus 1,500.

28
00:02:52,382 --> 00:02:57,790
Then it closed back at 2643. Now the sellers are trapped.

29
00:02:58,239 --> 00:03:02,524
When they exit they buy, and price can go up.

30
00:03:03,524 --> 00:03:11,882
The plan idea on the first example. Wait for a close back inside, entry around 2658.

31
00:03:12,333 --> 00:03:18,132
Stop above the trap high, at 2665.5. Risk 7.5 points.

32
00:03:18,582 --> 00:03:24,067
Target 2648, so 10 points. Around 1.3R.

33
00:03:25,067 --> 00:03:32,147
And when is the idea wrong? If price closes back above 2664, there was no trap. Get out.

34
00:03:32,597 --> 00:03:38,317
Not every failed breakout is a trap. Without big delta, few people are stuck.

35
00:03:38,768 --> 00:03:45,037
And in forex this delta is not real, only tick volume. The real picture is on futures.

36
00:03:46,037 --> 00:03:51,445
So, today in short. A trapped trader places the opposite order to get out.

37
00:03:51,894 --> 00:03:58,424
The face of a trap: big delta, no follow-through, close back inside, delta flips.

38
00:03:58,875 --> 00:04:04,361
Stop always beyond the trap. If price goes back, the idea is over.

39
00:04:05,361 --> 00:04:14,347
Next episode: failed auctions, single prints, and poor highs or lows. Signs of unfinished business in the profile.

40
